@prefix recon: <https://conceptkernel.org/ontology/v3.12/recon#> .
@prefix ckp:   <https://conceptkernel.org/ontology/v3.11/core#> .
@prefix sh:    <http://www.w3.org/ns/shacl#> .
@prefix rdf:   <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .
@prefix rdfs:  <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix owl:   <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix xsd:   <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

#######################################################################
# CKP v3.12 — RECON MODULE
#
# THE SMALLEST MODULE THAT PACKAGES AN AFFORDANCE. It exists to answer
# one question with an artifact instead of an argument: what is the
# minimum a concept kernel must ship to be adopted, gated, and callable?
#
# R7 IS NORMATIVE AND ENFORCED, NOT ADVISORY. ckp.import_module scans an
# incoming module for `ckp:X a rdfs:Class|owl:Class|owl:ObjectProperty|
# owl:DatatypeProperty` and REFUSES the whole file if it finds one:
#
#   "module mints term(s) into the CORE namespace — R7 is normative,
#    re-issue under domain naming and adopt it by digest."
#
# So this file REFERENCES core (ckp:Affordance, ckp:inTopic) and DECLARES
# nothing into it. Every term it mints is under recon:. That is the whole
# difference between a module and a fork.
#
# HOW A MODULE REACHES A SURFACE — measured on ck-allinone v0.7.37 /
# pgCK 0.4.81, not repeated from a spec:
#
#   1. the graph is PLACED in the store as urn:ckp:module:<name>
#      (a pgRDF act — the data plane; source_sha256 recorded per graph)
#   2. a ckp:Adoption is SEALED naming {adopts, intoProject, intoEpoch,
#      sourceDigest} (a pgCK act — the law plane)
#
# Step 2 alone is not enough and step 1 alone does nothing: proximity is
# not adoption. Both, or the types are refused as not admitted.
#
# ckp.import_module IS CLOSED BY RULING — its known-module set is
# ARRAY[]::text[] and its message is explicit: "Every module in force is
# adopted by digest (ckp:Adoption), not imported from a mount." It is
# retained as the R7 tripwire, not as a loader. Do not read its existence
# as a delivery path.
#
# THE OPEN GAP THIS FILE MAKES CONCRETE — ckp:derivedBy is minCount 1 AND
# maxCount 1 on AffordanceShape, and its range is a ckp:Materialization,
# which only exists AT AN EPOCH. A statically packaged affordance
# therefore cannot carry a true derivedBy at authoring time. That is not
# a flaw in this file; it is the declared/unsealed gap (#56) seen from
# the packaging side, and it is why ck_doctor reports
# `affordances NONE declared` on a kernel whose verbs route perfectly.
#
# This file states the affordance's CONTRACT and leaves derivedBy to be
# sealed by the adopting epoch. An adopter that cannot supply it should
# REFUSE and name this clause — never mint a placeholder Materialization,
# because a derivedBy pointing at a fabricated epoch is worse than an
# absent one: it is unfalsifiable provenance.
#######################################################################

#######################################################################
# §1  THE DATA — one type, its two properties, one shape that gates it.
#######################################################################

recon:Chunk a rdfs:Class ;
  rdfs:label "Chunk" ;
  rdfs:comment "A span of one document, carved on that document's OWN skeleton — a spec's numbered clause, an LLD's component — never a fixed-size window. Fixed windows destroy exactly the boundaries that carry meaning in these genres, so the carve is structural first and semantic only within an over-long clause." .

recon:text a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
  rdfs:domain recon:Chunk ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
  rdfs:comment "The span verbatim. Never a summary: prose is the least refusable artifact in this system, and a model-written summary that enters the record is fluent, unrefusable and authoritative by default." .

recon:section a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
  rdfs:domain recon:Chunk ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
  rdfs:comment "The span's address in its own document (e.g. \"§3.2\"). This is what makes a finding CITABLE rather than merely asserted — a claim about a chunk that cannot name where it came from is the filer's word." .

recon:ChunkShape a sh:NodeShape ;
  sh:targetClass recon:Chunk ;
  sh:property [ sh:path recon:text    ; sh:minCount 1 ; sh:maxCount 1 ; sh:datatype xsd:string ] ;
  sh:property [ sh:path recon:section ; sh:minCount 1 ; sh:maxCount 1 ; sh:datatype xsd:string ] ;
  sh:property [ sh:path rdfs:label    ; sh:maxCount 1 ; sh:datatype xsd:string ] .

#######################################################################
# §2  THE AFFORDANCE — the packaged operation.
#
# NEGATIVE CONTROL FOR THIS MODULE, and it must ship with it: seal a
# recon:Chunk carrying text but NO section. ChunkShape must REFUSE it on
# MinCount(1) naming recon:section. If that write succeeds, this module
# is decorative and the gate selected no focus node — check M4
# (ckp:conformsToShape) on the result before believing either outcome.
#######################################################################

recon:align a ckp:Affordance ;
  rdfs:label "recon.align" ;
  ckp:inTopic "recon.align" ;
  ckp:inShape recon:ChunkShape ;
  ckp:plane   "instance" ;
  rdfs:comment "Given two chunks, return the RELATION between them — never a merge. The three outcomes are kept apart on purpose: a COLLISION (two names, one concept) is resolvable by a governed equivalence; an EXTENSION (a name the surface does not declare) is resolvable by governed addition, or by nothing, because not every noun deserves a type; a CONFLICT (one name, two incompatible commitments) is NEVER merged — it is the finding itself, not a dedup opportunity. Conflating the third with the first is how a reconciliation quietly destroys the disagreement it was built to surface. NOTE: ckp:derivedBy is deliberately ABSENT here and is required by ckp:AffordanceShape — see the header. It is the adopting epoch's to seal, and an adopter that cannot must refuse and name the clause." .
